# Tailwind CSS Usage Rules for Next.js + Modus

## 🎯 Overview

This document outlines the proper usage of Tailwind CSS in Next.js applications with Modus Design System. The goal is to **maximize Tailwind utility classes** and **eliminate custom CSS classes** for better maintainability, consistency, and scalability.

**Key Principle**: Use Tailwind's built-in utilities (including `hover:`, `focus:`, `bg-*`, `text-*`, `border-*`) inline instead of creating custom CSS classes. This keeps your codebase lean and prevents `globals.css` from growing unmanageably.

## 🚫 NEVER Create Custom CSS Classes

**❌ DO NOT create custom CSS classes for styling:**

```css
/* ❌ NEVER DO THIS - Don't create custom classes */
.app-header {
  ...;
}
.nav-link {
  ...;
}
.card-bordered {
  ...;
}
.custom-button {
  ...;
}
```

**✅ ALWAYS use Tailwind inline:**

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Pure Tailwind
<header className="bg-background border-border text-foreground">
<Link className="hover:bg-card">
<div className="bg-card border border-border rounded-lg">
<button className="bg-primary text-primary-foreground">
```

## 🎨 Core Principles

### ✅ ALWAYS Use Tailwind Classes Inline (Preferred)

**Best Approach - Pure Tailwind with Design System Colors:**

```tsx
// ✅ BEST - Pure Tailwind with design system colors
<div className="flex items-center justify-between p-4 rounded-lg border bg-background border-border text-foreground">
  Content
</div>

// ✅ BEST - Hover states inline with Tailwind
<Link
  href="/page"
  className="px-4 py-2 rounded transition-colors duration-200 text-foreground hover:bg-card no-underline"
>
  Link
</Link>

// ✅ BEST - Header/Footer with design system colors
<header className="w-full border-b bg-background border-border text-foreground">
  <nav className="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 py-4">Navigation</nav>
</header>

<footer className="text-center py-4 border-t-2 bg-card border-border text-foreground">
  <div className="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4">Footer content</div>
</footer>
```

### Why This Approach?

1. **✅ No Custom CSS Bloat**: Tailwind purges unused classes automatically
2. **✅ Self-Documenting**: All styling visible in the component
3. **✅ Better DX**: No jumping between files to understand styles
4. **✅ Scalable**: Won't grow unmanageably like custom CSS
5. **✅ Maintainable**: Easy to modify inline
6. **✅ Design System Integration**: Works perfectly with Modus colors

### ❌ NEVER Create Custom CSS Classes for Styling

**Only create custom CSS for these 2 cases:**

1. **Theme-based logo/icon switching** (can't be done with Tailwind)
2. **Complex animations** requiring `@keyframes`

**That's it. Everything else uses Tailwind inline.**

```css
/* ❌ NEVER DO THIS - Don't create custom classes */
.custom-header {
  background-color: var(--background);
  border-color: var(--border);
  color: var(--foreground);
}

.card-bordered {
  background-color: var(--card);
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
}

.nav-link:hover {
  background-color: var(--card);
}

/* ✅ INSTEAD - Use Tailwind inline */
<header className="bg-background border-border text-foreground">
<div className="bg-card border border-border">
<Link className="hover:bg-card">
```

